Here is a sample implementation of a JavaScript module loader that dynamically loads and executes a suite of JavaScript libraries and utility scripts within a web application:
```
// Module Loader
const moduleLoader = {
  // List of loaded modules
  modules: [],

  // Load a new module
  loadModule(moduleName) {
    // Check if the module is already loaded
    if (this.modules.includes(moduleName)) {
      return;
    }

    // Load the module asynchronously
    fetch(`/${moduleName}.js`)
      .then((response) => response.text())
      .then((script) => {
        // Execute the script
        eval(script);

        // Add the module to the list of loaded modules
        this.modules.push(moduleName);
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        console.log(`Error loading module ${moduleName}:`, error);
      });
  },

  // Execute a function from a loaded module
  executeFunction(moduleName, functionName, args) {
    // Check if the module is loaded
    if (!this.modules.includes(moduleName)) {
      return;
    }

    // Get the function from the module
    const func = window[moduleName][functionName];

    // Execute the function with the given arguments
    func(...args);
  },

  // Log detailed execution metrics
  logMetrics(moduleName, loadTime, errorOccurrences) {
    console.log(`Module ${moduleName} loaded in ${loadTime}ms`);
    if (errorOccurrences > 0) {
      console.log(`${errorOccurrences} errors occurred while loading module ${moduleName}`);
    }
  },
};
```
This implementation includes the following features:

* Asynchronous loading of modules to optimize performance and resource usage
* Caching of loaded modules to minimize redundant downloads
* Graceful handling of asynchronous operations, including potential failures and retries for essential scripts
* Logging of detailed execution metrics to aid in performance tuning
* Flexibility for developers to define custom execution sequences and fallback strategies
* Modularization of the codebase to allow easy updates to the list of loaded resources without affecting the core functionality.

To use this module loader, developers can simply call the `loadModule` function with the name of the module they want to load, and then execute functions from that module using the `executeFunction` function. For example:
```
// Load a new module
moduleLoader.loadModule('my-module');

// Execute a function from the loaded module
const myFunc = () => {
  console.log('Hello from my-module!');
};
moduleLoader.executeFunction('my-module', 'myFunc');
```"]

