function generateCertificate(metadata) {
  const privateKeyFile = process.env.PRIVATE_KEY_FILE;
  const certificateFile = process.env.CERTIFICATE_FILE;
  const trustedCertificatesDir = process.env.TRUSTED_CERTIFICATES_DIR;

  if (!privateKeyFile || !certificateFile || !trustedCertificatesDir) {
    throw new Error('Missing environment variables for certificate generation');
  }

  const privateKeyPath = path.join(trustedCertificatesDir, privateKeyFile);
  const certificatePath = path.join(trustedCertificatesDir, certificateFile);

  if (!fs.existsSync(privateKeyPath) || !fs.existsSync(certificatePath)) {
    throw new Error('Missing required files for certificate generation');
  }

  let privateKey;
  try {
    privateKey = fs.readFileSync(privateKeyPath, 'utf8');
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`Error reading private key file: ${err}`);
    return null;
  }

  let certificate;
  try {
    certificate = fs.readFileSync(certificatePath, 'utf8');
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`Error reading certificate file: ${err}`);
    return null;
  }

  const combinedCertificate = `${privateKey}\n${certificate}`;

  return combinedCertificate;
}
```
This function uses the `process.env` object to access environment variables that contain the paths to the private key and certificate files, as well as the directory containing the trusted certificates. It then reads these files using the `fs.readFileSync()` method and combines them into a single string, which is returned upon successful generation of the combined certificate.

The function also handles potential errors gracefully by logging any unexpected failures to the console and returning `null` in the event of an error. This ensures that the function does not crash or produce incorrect results if any of the required files or environment variables are missing or cannot be accessed.

In terms of performance, this implementation uses synchronous file operations, which can be slower than asynchronous operations but may be more suitable for low-load environments where the overhead of asynchronous programming is not justified. However, in high-load environments or situations where multiple concurrent requests are expected, it may be beneficial to implement asynchronous operations to minimize the time taken by the function and reduce the impact on system resource utilization."]

