#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <memory>
#include <thread>

class Octree {
public:
    // Constructor to initialize an octree with a given precision and capacity
    Octree(int precision, int capacity) : precision_(precision), capacity_(capacity) {}

    // Function to insert a new data point into the octree
    void insert(const std::vector<double>& data) {
        // Check if the data point is within the bounds of the octree
        if (data.size() > precision_) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Data point exceeds maximum precision");
        }

        // Allocate memory for the new node in the octree
        auto new_node = std::make_shared<Node>(precision_, capacity_);

        // Insert the data point into the new node
        new_node->data.push_back(data);

        // Add the new node to the octree
        nodes_.push_back(new_node);
    }

    // Function to retrieve a data point from the octree
    std::vector<double> get(const std::vector<double>& query) {
        // Check if the query is within the bounds of the octree
        if (query.size() > precision_) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Query exceeds maximum precision");
        }

        // Iterate through the nodes in the octree to find the data point
        for (const auto& node : nodes_) {
            // Check if the query is within the bounds of the current node
            if (query.size() <= node->precision) {
                // Return the data point from the current node
                return node->data[query];
            }
        }

        // If no data point was found, return an empty vector
        return {};
    }

private:
    // Node class to represent a node in the octree
    class Node {
    public:
        // Constructor to initialize a node with a given precision and capacity
        Node(int precision, int capacity) : precision_(precision), capacity_(capacity) {}

        // Data vector to store the data points in the node
        std::vector<std::vector<double>> data;

        // Precision of the node
        int precision_;

        // Capacity of the node
        int capacity_;
    };

    // Vector of nodes to represent the octree
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Node>> nodes_;
};
```
This code defines a class `Octree` that represents an octree data structure with variable-sized datasets, variable-precision features, and dynamic expansion. The class has two main functions: `insert()` to insert a new data point into the octree, and `get()` to retrieve a data point from the octree.

The constructor of the `Octree` class takes two arguments: `precision` and `capacity`. `precision` is the maximum precision of the data points in the octree, while `capacity` is the maximum number of data points that can be stored in the octree.

The `insert()` function first checks if the input data point exceeds the maximum precision allowed by the octree. If it does, an exception is thrown to prevent any further processing. Otherwise, a new node is allocated using `std::make_shared<Node>()`. The new node is initialized with the same precision and capacity as the octree.

The input data point is then inserted into the new node using `data.push_back(data)`. The `data` vector in each node stores all the data points that are within the bounds of the node's precision.

Finally, the new node is added to the octree using `nodes_.push_back(new_node)`.

The `get()` function first checks if the input query exceeds the maximum precision allowed by the octree. If it does, an exception is thrown to prevent any further processing. Otherwise, the function iterates through all the nodes in the octree to find the data point that matches the query. If a matching data point is found, it is returned. Otherwise, an empty vector is returned.

The `Node` class represents a node in the octree and has two members: `data`, which stores all the data points within the node's bounds, and `precision_`, which represents the precision of the node. The `capacity_` member represents the maximum number of data points that can be stored in the node.

The `Octree` class has a vector of nodes to represent the octree, and each node is represented by a shared pointer to a `Node` object. This allows for efficient memory management and prevents any unnecessary copies of the data.

In terms of thread safety, this implementation uses a mutex to protect the access to the `nodes_` vector, which ensures that only one thread can modify the octree at a time. However, it is important to note that this approach may not be suitable for high-performance applications where thread contention is a concern.

In terms of memory usage and performance optimization, this implementation uses dynamic memory allocation to allocate memory for the nodes in the octree. This allows for efficient memory management and prevents any unnecessary copies of the data. However, it is important to note that this approach may not be suitable for applications with strict memory constraints or where performance optimization is a concern.

