The function then generates a SHA-256 hash of the original file name using the `SHA256` function from the `openssl` library. It gets the current Unix timestamp using the `time` function and generates a sequence of digits based on the first 10 characters of the original file name, excluding any non-alphanumeric characters. The sequence is generated by copying the first 8 characters of the original file name into a new string and then padding it with zeros if necessary to ensure that it has a length of 8 characters.

Finally, the function builds the new file name with enhanced metadata using the `sprintf` function, which formats the output according to a format string. The format string includes the directory part of the original path, the base name of the original file without its extension, the SHA-256 hash of the original file name, the current Unix timestamp, and the sequence of digits generated earlier. The new file name is then returned as a dynamically allocated string using the `malloc` function.

Note that this code assumes that the input path is valid and that the directory part of the path exists. It also assumes that the predefined list of allowed extensions is not empty and contains at least one element. If these assumptions are not met, the behavior of the code is undefined.']

