The function uses a fixed-size buffer to read the lines from the file, which reduces the amount of memory allocated by the program. However, this approach can lead to performance bottlenecks if the file contains very large numbers or if there are many lines in the file. In such cases, using a dynamically allocated array can help improve the performance by reducing the number of reallocations required.

The function also uses a helper function to calculate the mean and standard deviation of the numbers. This approach helps reduce the computational complexity of the algorithm, as it avoids having to iterate over the entire array multiple times to calculate these values.

Overall, this implementation is robust against potential performance bottlenecks and can handle large datasets efficiently. The trade-offs between using a fixed-size buffer versus a dynamically allocated one are evaluated based on factors such as memory usage and computation time, with the goal of finding the optimal solution for the specific use case.']

