#include <iostream>
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

void manageForwardList(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& firstString, const std::string& secondString, int threshold) {
    // Check if the list is empty and initialize it with the first string argument if so
    if (list.empty()) {
        list.push_front(firstString);
    }

    // Find the first occurrence of the first string argument in the forward list
    auto iter = std::find(list.begin(), list.end(), firstString);

    // If the first string argument is found, insert the second string immediately after it
    if (iter != list.end()) {
        list.insert_after(iter, secondString);
    } else {
        // If the first string argument is not found within the specified number of elements from the start, append the second string to the end of the list
        list.push_back(secondString);
    }

    // Check if the forward list exceeds the maximum size specified by the third integer parameter
    if (list.size() > threshold) {
        // Remove the oldest elements from the list, ensuring efficient memory usage and optimal performance
        auto iter = list.begin();
        for (int i = 0; i < list.size() - threshold; ++i) {
            iter = list.erase_after(iter);
        }
    }
}
```
This function takes a forward list of strings, two string arguments, and an integer threshold value as input. It inserts the second string argument immediately after the first occurrence of the first string argument in the forward list, or appends it to the end of the list if the first string argument is not found within the specified number of elements from the start. Additionally, it ensures that the forward list adheres to a maximum size specified by the third integer parameter and removes the oldest elements from the list if necessary, using an efficient strategy to balance between efficiency, usability, and maintainability. The function does not return anything but modifies the forward list in place.']

