The code also defines a `MAC_TRANSACTION_T` structure that stores a valid `MAC_PDU`, `SAP`, and `PDCP_PDU`. The `enqueue()` function allocates memory for the new transaction using `malloc()`, while the `dequeue()` function frees the memory allocated for the oldest transaction.

The code uses a linked list to manage the queue, which allows for efficient insertion and deletion of transactions at the end of the queue. The `sem_wait()` and `sem_post()` functions are used to synchronize access to the queue using a mutex, which ensures that only one thread can access the queue at a time.

The code also includes robust error handling for memory allocation failures by checking the return value of `malloc()` and freeing the memory allocated for the oldest transaction if it fails. The function can gracefully degrade under high-load conditions without losing data integrity by using a fixed-size array to manage the queue, which allows for efficient insertion and deletion of transactions at the end of the queue.

