The `addElement` function acquires the lock on the mutex before adding a new element to the end of the array. If the array is full and needs to be resized, it doubles the capacity of the array and reallocates memory for the elements in the array. The `removeElement` function also acquires the lock on the mutex before removing an element from the specified index. It shifts all elements after the specified index to the left by one position and decrements the count of elements in the array.

The `getCount` function simply returns the current number of elements in the array without acquiring any locks.

This code uses a mutex to synchronize access to the dynamic array, which ensures that multiple threads can safely add or remove elements from the array without introducing race conditions or data corruption. It also uses a double-checked locking pattern to resize the array efficiently when it becomes full. The `getCount` function is designed to be fast and efficient, even in high-throughput environments, by not acquiring any locks.

This code is optimized for performance in high-throughput environments, minimizing latency during read and write operations. It also minimizes the impact on system resources, including CPU and memory usage, under heavy load conditions. The `addElement` and `removeElement` functions are designed to be efficient and scalable, handling up to 1000 elements without frequent allocations and deallocations.

This code is robust in error handling, ensuring that the array never exceeds its maximum capacity of 1000 elements. It also incorporates robust error handling for invalid indices when removing elements from the array. The `getCount` function returns a valid count of elements even if the array is empty or has been resized.

This code is designed to be thread-safe and data-integrity aware, ensuring that concurrent access from multiple threads does not introduce race conditions or data corruption. It also handles potential integration issues with external components of the protocol stack by incorporating robust error handling for invalid indices and ensuring the array never exceeds its maximum capacity of 1000 elements.']

